{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789502116,"nonce":"QAQDlOI0LZEbzPbVhTdQ8U55/GI=","title":"List temporaries from growing builders and loop-reassigned lists are left to the collector","message":"In fannkuch's flip loop the slice temporary (`perm[k::-1]`) is never released by the analysis: `zb_list_slice_i64` grows its result by push, and `returns_owned_storage` admits only a function holding exactly one allocation, so the result is not a site at all. `perm = perm1[:]` inside the loop is a phi the analysis cannot own either (\"cannot own incoming\"). The program makes about 10 million such blocks and the collector runs 24 times, each finding nothing reached and freeing 16 MB of pure garbage in under 0.2 ms; measured on the M1 Pro the churn costs under 10 ms of 139 (a smaller floor recycles blocks sooner and changes nothing), so this is about the allocation pattern rather than the collector.\n\nTwo things to widen: a builder whose only allocations are its result's header and the growth of that result's storage returns owned storage; and a loop-carried list reassigned from an owned site each iteration can be released on the back edge before the next assignment. The second list of a two-list operation (`extend`, `concat`, `assign_slice`, comparisons) is now `borrowed`, so a literal or slice passed straight in is released after the call.","files":null}]}